# /loop runner template (Claude Code)

`/loop` re-invokes a prompt on an interval. For V1, use `/loop` only as the
Claude scheduler surface: each wake must run the guarded shell runner once and
then stop. The guarded runner owns trust, claim, Git writes, proof, PRs, labels,
markers, and nested Claude edit/review calls.

Start Claude Code from the target repo with permissions that allow this one
runner command to execute unattended, for example:

```sh
claude --permission-mode bypassPermissions --add-dir "{{repo_path}}"
```

Create one `/loop` entry per role:

```text
/loop every {{interval}}: Run exactly this command once, then stop:

{{repo_path}}/.agent-loops/runners/claude.sh "{{repo_path}}" {{role}}

Do not load the autonomous-work-loops skill directly. Do not reconstruct the workflow yourself. Do not run gh or git directly except through that runner. Do not install cron jobs. Do not create additional scheduled loops. If the command reports no eligible work, report the no-op and stop.
```

Roles: `implementer`, `reviewer`, `fixer`.

The external cost wall (ADR-0007): `/loop` itself does not impose a
per-iteration OS timeout. The guarded `claude.sh` runner wraps nested Claude
calls with `timeout`, `gtimeout`, or the shell fallback from the repo config.
